Technically speaking, there is no limit to the amount of enchantment a 
weapon or piece of armor can take.  However, ...

Let's discuss both enchanting weapons and enchanting armor.

SPOILER ALERT

 
Enchant weapon scroll:

A blessed scroll will increase a weapon's enchantment by 3.  An uncursed
scroll will increase it by 1.  A cursed one will decrease it by 1.  If a
weapon starts out at > +5 (i.e., is currently +6 or more) when you read a
blessed or uncursed enchant weapon scroll, there is a 2/3 chance of it
evaporating.  This works similiarly, but in reverse, for disenchanting. 
If a weapon starts out at < -5 when you read a cursed enchant weapon
scroll, there is a 2/3 change that it will evaporate, too.

Bottom line:  If it's +5 or less, you can use a blessed enchant weapon
scroll to get to +8 with no risk.  If it's +6 or more, it's very risky to
go further.


Enchant armor scroll:

A blessed scroll will increase a piece of armor's enchantment by a random
amount of from 1 to 3.  An uncursed scroll will increase it by 1.  A
cursed one will decrease it by 1.  If the armor is > +3 (> +5 for elven
mithril) when you read a blessed or uncursed enchant armor scroll, there
is a (1 - (1/current enchantment)) chance of it evaporating.  Thus, for
example, if it's currently +4, there's a 75% chance of it evaporating;
if it's +5, there's an 80% change of losing it; and so on.

Bottom line:  If it's +3 or less (+5 or less for elven mithril), you can
use a blessed enchant armor scroll with no risk.  If it's +4 or more (+6
or more for elven mithril), you have a high probability of losing it.


Note:  A character's "luck" does not factor into determining the
probability of evaporation (or the amount of enchantment for a blessed
enchant armor scroll).  You're strictly at the mercy of the pseudo-random
number generator. 


